      "seed": 1436923214,
      "version": 920,
      "versionNonce": 286834066,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785089492642,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "• Auto-scaling replica based on traffic load\n• Blockchain operations (Send Tx, Tx History, Accounts)\n• DB-persisted replica of the entire blockchain ledger",
      "fontSize": 12.371181355870196,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "• Auto-scaling replica based on traffic load\n• Blockchain operations (Send Tx, Tx History, Accounts)\n• DB-persisted replica of the entire blockchain ledger",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b0W",
      "autoResize": false
    },
    {
      "id": "HXgjNrQEN7dMgOZwtjRrd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 77.64369957905672,
      "y": 944.7008257941703,
      "width": 819.9378679281759,
      "height": 55.972619741141315,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1749878350,
      "version": 1445,
      "versionNonce": 419577742,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785089478897,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "• Managed Instance Group - Support for rolling updates and rollback of faulty deployments\n• Full network segregation via Virtual Private Network - Accessible only from the Stellar Horizon instance \n• Double persistency via Virtual Disks and external DB instance",
      "fontSize": 14.926031930971018,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "• Managed Instance Group - Support for rolling updates and rollback of faulty deployments\n• Full network segregation via Virtual Private Network - Accessible only from the Stellar Horizon instance \n• Double persistency via Virtual Disks and external DB instance",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b0X",
      "autoResize": false
    },
    {
      "id": "TyYkDQUC_RQIrdSfgOFXT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -177.96743723712723,
      "y": 837.2768988088274,
      "width": 6.399993896484375,
      "height": 20,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#6741d9",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0Y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1297561746,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 615204366,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785089666070,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5aruTUD5GA8B4uohLeTCJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -642.3599586523569,
      "y": 138.89374103492986,
      "width": 599.6278179866765,
      "height": 862.6388194849255,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 792657490,
      "version": 1797,
      "versionNonce": 37575822,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785090062862,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Large enterprises that operate through a network of partners\nface a recurring problem: how do you let partners run their own\npayment ecosystems while keeping everything settled on a shared,\ntrusted ledger? Traditional payment rails are slow, opaque, and not\ndesigned for this kind of multi-party setup. A public blockchain works\ndifferently but brings its own headaches — transaction fees, key\nmanagement complexity, and exposure to network congestion you\ncan't control.\n\n301chain was built to solve that specific problem by running a\nprivate fork of the Stellar blockchain. The choice of Stellar made\nsense: it's designed around asset issuance and trustlines, so\nmodelling partner-specific tokens that users can hold, spend, and\nexchange is a natural fit rather than a workaround.\n\nThe core flow was straightforward. A client lands on a partner's\nplatform, pays in FIAT, and receives tokens. Those tokens are\npartner-scoped — they live and spend within that partner's\necosystem. But they're not trapped there: they can be swapped for\nanother partner's tokens, or redeemed back to FIAT. The whole\ncircuit is closed, meaning the main entity controls and guarantees\nevery settlement without relying on an external network.\n\nBecause this was a private chain, some of Stellar's public-network\nassumptions had to be stripped away or abstracted. There are no\ntransaction fees in a private closed circuit, so the server handled\nfee sponsoring transparently via treasury accounts. Key custody\nwas pluggable, with Fireblocks as the primary provider. The goal was\nthat integrating partners would never have to think about\ntrustlines, key pairs, or fee mechanics at all.\n\nThe three-layer infrastructure reflects how much operational\nseriousness went into it: consensus handled by three validator\nnodes with rolling deployments, a managed Horizon instance as the\nAPI surface, and the 301chain server sitting above both to present\na clean, blockchain-agnostic interface. The ambition was to\neventually plug in other chains underneath the same abstraction —\nthat part never shipped, but the design left the door open.\n",
      "fontSize": 17.695155271485653,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Large enterprises that operate through a network of partners face a recurring problem: how do you let partners run their own payment ecosystems while keeping everything settled on a shared, trusted ledger? Traditional payment rails are slow, opaque, and not designed for this kind of multi-party setup. A public blockchain works differently but brings its own headaches — transaction fees, key management complexity, and exposure to network congestion you can't control.\n\n301chain was built to solve that specific problem by running a private fork of the Stellar blockchain. The choice of Stellar made sense: it's designed around asset issuance and trustlines, so modelling partner-specific tokens that users can hold, spend, and exchange is a natural fit rather than a workaround.\n\nThe core flow was straightforward. A client lands on a partner's platform, pays in FIAT, and receives tokens. Those tokens are partner-scoped — they live and spend within that partner's ecosystem. But they're not trapped there: they can be swapped for another partner's tokens, or redeemed back to FIAT. The whole circuit is closed, meaning the main entity controls and guarantees every settlement without relying on an external network.\n\nBecause this was a private chain, some of Stellar's public-network assumptions had to be stripped away or abstracted. There are no transaction fees in a private closed circuit, so the server handled fee sponsoring transparently via treasury accounts. Key custody was pluggable, with Fireblocks as the primary provider. The goal was that integrating partners would never have to think about trustlines, key pairs, or fee mechanics at all.\n\nThe three-layer infrastructure reflects how much operational seriousness went into it: consensus handled by three validator nodes with rolling deployments, a managed Horizon instance as the API surface, and the 301chain server sitting above both to present a clean, blockchain-agnostic interface. The ambition was to eventually plug in other chains underneath the same abstraction — that part never shipped, but the design left the door open.\n",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b0Z",
      "autoResize": false
    },
    {
      "id": "K2LODydGEDMuk-7E81F6J",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -488.4986744201731,
      "y": 76.14401586344599,
      "width": 251.99546813964844,
      "height": 38.36815098258504,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
